course: dictionary
lesson: 14231
title: Sounds through the nose
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To nasalise a sound is to let air come out of your nose as well as your mouth. Try it: say «m» and pinch your nose. The sound feels buzzy because air is going through your nose. That is nasalising.
explain: The sounds «m», «n» and «ng» are nasal sounds. You can feel air at your nose when you say them. Sounds like «p» and «t» keep the air in your mouth, so they are not nasal.
explain: French does this with vowels. In words like «bon» and «vin», the vowel is nasalised, so part of the air goes out through the nose. This is why French words can sound different from English words.
explain: A blocked nose, like when you have a cold, can make your voice sound less nasal. Less air can get through your nose, so the sound changes.
fact: Nasalise is a verb: I nasalise, he nasalises, they nasalised, we are nasalising.
fact: «Nasalise» means to make a speech sound through the nose, letting air come out of the nose as well as the mouth.
fact: «m», «n» and «ng» are nasal sounds.
fact: French has nasalised vowels, as in «bon» and «vin».
fact: A cold can block your nose, so your voice may sound less nasal.
exercise: «The teacher asked us to nasalise the sound m and feel the air at our nose.» What does nasalise mean here? | Close your mouth and make no sound | Whisper very softly | Sing the note very loudly | * Let air come out through the nose as well as the mouth
exercise: Which of these is a nasal sound? | p | t | k | * n
exercise: «French words like bon and vin have nasalised vowels.» Which language is this about? | Japanese | * French | Spanish | Dutch
test: What does «nasalise» mean? | Say a sound very fast | * Make a sound come out through the nose as well as the mouth | Hold your lips tightly closed and stop the sound | Make a sound with no voice at all
test: «When Sam has a cold, his voice sounds less nasal.» Why? | * His nose is blocked, so less air can go through it | His mouth is too wide | He is whispering | He is speaking in French
exam: Which word best describes what happens when you nasalise a sound? | * Letting air flow through the nose | Silence | Shouting | Dropping your voice
